6f85d45b-596b-40e7-809e-6fad8abadb76,"West, Civil war, Puppets, Colour revolutions, Conspiracy theory, Nazi/Fascist",1e3744b0-bbfc-506a-832e-b527e5f5b64e,alphahistory.com,alphahistory.com,https://alphahistory.com/russianrevolution/russian-civil-war/,English,07-12-2019,trustworthy,"The Russian Civil War (1918-21) was fought for control of Russia after the October Revolution. A conglomerate of anti-Bolshevik groups, loosely dubbed the Whites, sought to remove the Bolsheviks from power and restore at least some elements of the old order. Other groups not directly aligned with either the Bolsheviks or Whites fought for control or independence in their own regions. The Russian Civil War was a pervasive conflict that involved disparate political and military groups, nationalist movements and all classes of Russian society. Foreign nations opposed to the Bolshevik regime also contributed troops, weapons, supplies and intelligence. Like other internecine conflicts, the Russian Civil War would produce confusion, division, attack and retaliation, intense propaganda, war crimes and human suffering on catastrophic levels.
Background
The Russian Civil War began from strong and widespread resistance to the new order in Russia. Opposition to the Bolsheviks after October 1917 was considerable. By early 1918, this opposition had begun to strengthen and intensify into a genuine counter-revolutionary movement. Tsarists, liberals, Mensheviks and Socialist-Revolutionaries all considered the Bolsheviks usurpers who had stolen power, whatever their claims of representing the working classes.
The catalyst for civil war was an uprising by the Czech Legion, sometimes called the Czechoslovak Corps. Formed in 1914, the Czech Legion was a Russian Imperial Army unit comprised of around 40,000-strong unit volunteers of Central European heritage. Most were Czech and Slovak nationalists hostile to the Austro-Hungarians that ruled their country. Some were former conscripts in the Austro-Hungarian army who had been taken prisoner by the Russians and volunteered to switch sides.
After the October Revolution and the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk, the Czech Legion sought approval to leave Russia, via Vladivostok, so that it could sail to France to fight on the Western Front. Wary of the Legion’s counter-revolutionary potential, the Bolsheviks approved the request. The Legion began moving east but by May 1918 found itself scattered along the Trans-Siberian Railway and unable to move, due to critical transport problems and Bolshevik administrative barriers. Relations between Czech Legion soldiers and Soviet government officials, which were not amicable to begin with, began to deteriorate.
The Czech Legion rebels
By this point, the Bolshevik hierarchy was becoming concerned about the activities of the Czech Legion. On May 14th, the Czechs were involved in a skirmish at Ekaterinburg, and war minister Leon Trotsky ordered their arrest and disarmament. The legion refused to surrender its weapons and rebelled instead, killing numerous Bolshevik officials and soldiers and seizing control of Chelyabinsk.
Over the coming weeks, the Czech Legion expanded its revolt further. Bolshevik authority in the region was thin so the Czechs enjoyed much success, seizing control of territory east of the Volga River and several towns and stations along the Trans-Siberian railway. Along the way, they were joined by other groups hostile to the Bolsheviks, such as former tsarist officers and loyalist militias.
By the end of June 1918, the Czech Legion and other counter-revolutionaries controlled most of the Trans-Siberian railway all the way to, and including, the port city of Vladivostok. Their advance on the city of Ekaterinburg was a significant factor in the execution of Tsar Nicholas II in mid-July. Their early successes, along with the capture of Vladivostok and the Siberian railway, also encouraged Allied governments to intervene in the Russian Civil War.
Foreign intervention
After the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in March 1918, the Allies painted the Bolsheviks not only as traitors to the war cause but also, an existential threat to democracy and capitalism. Most foreign powers refused to recognise the legitimacy of the Bolshevik regime, instead dealing with White generals as government leaders in exile.
Through the remainder of 1918 into early 1919, Allied troops were dispatched to five different theatres of war across Russia. The largest contingent was from Japan, 70,000 men landing in Vladivostok or through the Chinese border and linking with the Czech Legion in Siberia. Some 40,000 British and Commonwealth troops were deployed in the Caucasus, east of the Black Sea, while 15,000 French troops landed in Ukraine. A similar-sized American force, along with smaller contingents of Canadians and Australians, served in Arkhangelsk in North Russia.
Around 200,000 foreign troops were deployed in Russia during the Civil War. In military terms, their involvement was lukewarm. It was rare for foreign soldiers to directly engage the Bolsheviks, and as a result, their casualties were comparatively low. Some foreign powers were chiefly interested in securing territory, providing safe passage for refugees and protecting resources previously loaned to Russia.
At the end of World War I in November 1918, war-weary nations had no appetite for committing large troop numbers to another major conflict. As a consequence, foreign troops began to withdraw from Russia in 1919. With the exception of the Japanese, who held their positions in the far east, all Allied troops had left Russia by 1920.
The White armies
The Czech Legion’s successful struggles against Bolshevik rule inspired the formation of other counter-revolutionary units across Russia. Collectively, these anti-Bolshevik forces became known as the White armies. Behind these armies was a political movement – a loose coalition of monarchists, liberals, non-Bolshevik socialists and disgruntled peasants. They had few shared values or objectives, other than opposition to the Bolsheviks and a determination to overthrow the Soviet regime.
Most White leaders, military or civilian, were nationalist and imperialist – they wanted to retain the Russian Empire and restore it to a position of strength in Europe and Asia. Beyond that, the movement had disparate elements with little in common. They did not agree on a single system of government, they had no planned foreign policy, no single leader or leadership group. Some wanted the tsar restored, some wanted a constitutional monarchy, some harked for a return of the Constituent Assembly, while others sought a republic. Indeed, some expressed no views on the matter at all.
As might be expected, the largest challenges faced by the White movement were division and disunity. There was also the logistical problem of organising, coordinating and communicating with numerous White forces dotted around the vast Russian landmass but separated by the enemy. This was in stark comparison to the Bolsheviks who, for much of the Russian Civil War, controlled Russia’s farming and industrial heartlands in Europe.
White commanders
The largest White armies were led by some prominent military men, all of them high-ranking tsarist officers who had held significant military posts during World War I.
In southern Russia, General Anton Denikin led the Volunteer Army, the largest of the White armies, reaching a peak of 40,000 soldiers in mid-1919. Denikin’s army was active in Ukraine and the Crimea, the Don Valley and the Caucasus. They won control of the Kuban region, east of the Black Sea, in 1918. The following July, Denikin and his troops attempted to capture Moscow, reaching within 220 miles (350 kilometres) of the Bolshevik capital.
Operating in the Baltic states, General Nicolai Yudenich’s Northwest army grew to as many as 25,000 men in size. In 1918, the Northwest army was given harbour and support by Estonia, which was seeking to protect itself from Bolshevik invasion. In 1919, Yudenich and his troops advanced west from Estonia, capturing Pskov and then Yamburg (now Kingisepp), just 80 miles (125 kilometres) from Petrograd.
Further east, in the Siberian city of Omsk, Admiral Alexander Kolchak headed not only a White army but a de facto military dictatorship. Recognised both by foreign governments and other White generals as the head of Russia’s government, Kolchak was the closest the White movement had to a single leader. In reality, his Siberian base was too distant and disconnected from other civil war theatres for Kolchak to have much impact.
The propaganda war
The Russian Civil War was not only waged over territory and resources – it was also a war for the hearts and minds of the Russian people. Because of this, the Bolsheviks and Whites made extensive use of propaganda, both to promote their own political cause and to demonise and ridicule the opposition. Because of Russia’s low literacy levels, most of this propaganda was visual and usually took the form of posters, art or cartoons.
There were some similarities and crossovers in Red and White propaganda. For example, both frequently employed scenes of famine, devastation or near apocalypse to depict a future Russia ruled by their enemies (see image above). Civil War propaganda relied heavily on symbols familiar to Russian workers and peasants: tsarist motifs like crowns, other class symbols like hammers and sickles, religious iconography, horses and dragons, heroic figures, caricatured enemies and, occasionally, anti-Semitic tropes.
The Bolsheviks held the clear upper hand in the propaganda war. While Soviet propagandists routinely attacked the Whites as tsarists and reactionaries, they were also able to provide an optimistic vision of Russia under their rule. Soviet posters hailed the working classes and promoted them as an integral part of Russia’s future. Progress in industry, literacy, workers’ rights and the status of women were all featured. These were issues the Whites could not agree on, let alone present in their propaganda.
Towards a conclusion
The year 1919 was a decisive one in the Russian Civil War. As mentioned above, the Volunteer Army and Northwest Army both engaged in major offensives against Bolshevik positions. These campaigns captured some significant territory, however, the Whites lacked the personnel, supplies and popular support to consolidate their gains and continue their advances.
In north-western Russia, a Red Army counterattack forced Yudenich’s army back inside the borders of Estonia, where it was held for several months. In the last weeks of 1919, the Estonian government made a peace deal with Moscow that resulted in the Northwest army being disarmed and several thousand of its officers and soldiers interned.
Near Moscow, Denikin’s advance on the capital had failed by October. Suffering losses and supply shortages, his Volunteer Army retreated to the south-east and was forced back as far as the Don Valley, then the Crimea. Admiral Kolchak was also in retreat by this time, surrendering Omsk to the Red Army in November 1919 and fleeing east to Irkutsk. He was captured by Bolshevik troops in January 1920 and executed by firing squad the following month.
Why the Bolsheviks won
The Bolshevik victory in the Russian Civil War can be attributed to several factors. The Whites were a divided and uncoordinated opposition that struggled to find adequate men, munitions and supplies. In contrast, the Soviet government maintained control of Russia’s critical industries, most of its major cities and its significant ports and railways. This provided them with access to infrastructure, communications and supply lines.
The Whites, in contrast, occupied and controlled large areas but mainly on the periphery. Despite being extensively armed and aided by the Allies, particularly the British, they routinely found themselves short of supplies. Any attack on Bolshevik-controlled European Russia would mean advancing several hundreds of miles, requiring troops, transportation and supply lines the Whites simply did not have.
In addition, the White armies had very capable military commanders but they were not good political leaders. Men like Kolchak and Denikin drew decent numbers of military recruits but they were unable to present an appealing alternative to Soviet rule and win the support of ordinary Russians. Indeed, while Kolchak talked of restoring the Constituent Assembly, his assumed title, ‘Supreme Leader of Russia’, harked back to tsarist autocracy.
Despite pockets of continued resistance, chiefly in the Crimea and the Japanese-occupied far east, the Civil War was virtually over by the last half of 1920. The total military casualties numbered around 800,000 but the civilian deaths from fighting, famine and disease numbered in the millions. The Bolsheviks emerged victorious but the gargantuan task of recovering and rebuilding Russia’s economic and social order lay ahead of them.
Five key points
1. The Russian Civil War erupted in 1918 when the Imperial Army’s Czech Legion, frustrated by its inability to leave Russia to join World War I, rebelled against the Bolsheviks near Ekaterinburg.
2. The Czech Legion captured vast swathes of territory along the Trans-Siberian railway, inspiring others to take up arms against the Bolsheviks. Collectively, they became known as the White armies.
3. There were several significant White armies in Siberia, southern and north-western Russia, led by capable military commanders like Kolchak, Denikin and Yudenich and aided by foreign governments.
4. The Whites, geographically divided and lacking cohesion and coordination, achieved some victories but found it difficult to make inroads into Bolshevik-held European Russia.
5. Ultimately, the Whites were unable to provide a clear vision of Russia under their rule or present themselves as an alternative to the Soviet government. By late 1920, their struggle was all but over.",The Russian Civil War
