Larissa said that she was born in Russia’s Far East, but had lived in Crimea for 37 years. “Russian troops seized the parliament building and hoisted a Russian flag. The whole referendum is taking place at the point of a Kalashnikov. It’s improper, and organised by Moscow.” She said she didn’t know how long she would be stay out of Crimea, but said she wanted to return for her grandson’s impending birthday.
Danchuck, her husband Taras, and their one-year-old son Lyubomyr had driven to the Maidan in a black saloon car decorated with anti-Putin slogans. One read: “Crimea=Ukraine”. Another described the Russian leader as an “executioner”. Lyubomyr sat placidly in his pushchair, wearing a romper suit and a yellow and blue scarf, above a sign which read: “Putin is a poo”.
