How We Rate
We record which authority licenses each operator and whether the license number is published and checkable. We separate international licensing from NCSLC authorization throughout, because accepting a customer in this state is not the same as being licensed to serve one here.
We look at standard pricing on spreads and totals, whether reduced juice is offered and on which leagues, and how margin compounds across parlay legs. For a high-volume bettor, a persistent -105 line is worth more than most welcome bonuses.
College basketball drives more local volume than anything else, so we weigh depth on NCAA markets heavily — player props, first-half lines, alternate spreads and conference futures — alongside NFL, NBA, MLB, NHL and NASCAR coverage.
We read past the dollar figure to the rollover multiple, whether it applies to bonus alone or deposit plus bonus, minimum odds, market exclusions, expiry windows and maximum cashout caps. A large match with a heavy obligation often beats nothing.
We check which cards and cryptocurrencies are supported, whether withdrawal channels mirror deposit channels, free withdrawal allowances, fees, and both per-transaction and weekly payout ceilings. A low weekly cap matters far more than most bettors expect.
We note what documents each operator requires, when they are requested, and whether the policy is disclosed up front or only at first withdrawal. Identity checks apply at internationally licensed books too — no operator of standing skips them.
We assess how each platform performs on iOS and Android, whether it runs as a responsive site or an installable progressive web app, live betting responsiveness, cashier navigation and biometric login. We never describe a browser portal as a native app store app.
We check for deposit, wager and loss limits, session timers, cool-off periods and self-exclusion, and how easily they can be found. We also note where self-exclusion does and does not carry across sister sites or the statewide program.
This guide covers operator reviews, promo codes, banking options, payout timing, tax basics, the difference between state-licensed and offshore books, and the responsible gambling tools and helplines available here. You must be 21 or older to wager on sports in this state.
