It was a fitting end to the tragedy or farce (depending on your point of view) of the Brexit imbroglio that the default position of Britain leaving without a deal on April 12, 2019 was destroyed by a single vote in parliament this week, with that vote belonging to a convict on license wearing an electronic tag. The matter now goes to the House of Lords, where members assembled in their ermine coats, not one of them elected, will determine the fate of the nation (albeit not on a Friday). Is this a fitting morality tale for the state of British democracy? We invited journalist, broadcaster, and political commentator Patrick Christys into the Sputnik studio to find out.
