âWe usually look at the profit-loss balance,â said Romas Å vedas, lecturer at Vilnius University, an energy expert, and a former Lithuanian energy vice minister. âBut we shouldnât calculate it the same way here, because economic and national security is a big part of the [LNG] terminalâs benefits.â
Just recently, LNG transported by tanker ships cost more than pipeline gas, but the situation is changing.
âThe view that we should in no circumstances [buy] Russian gas via the terminal has its limitations,â according to a Lithuanian MP and member of the Seimasâ Energy Committee, Virgilijus Poderys. âIf we enter the market to buy gas and immediately restrict ourselves, the prices will be automatically higher.â
Lack of regional solidarity
However, the running costs could be decreased if the other Baltic states were to get involved, said MP Poderys.
