f36ff60f-ab82-44cb-8e3c-b76dd21148cd,"migration, gas, Energy, Economic difficulties, Sovereignty",8c13442d-f97d-5a96-909a-d05417b2c7ba,worldstopexports.com,worldstopexports.com,http://www.worldstopexports.com/polands-top-import-partners/,English,05-07-2019,trustworthy,"Nicknamed Polonia (Land of the Fields), the Republic of Poland exported US$413.9 billion worth of products around the world in 2025.
That dollar amount results from a 30.2% upturn compared to five years earlier in 2021 when Polish exports totaled $317.8 billion.
Year over year, the value of Poland’s exports rose by 8.8% starting from $380.3 billion during 2024.
The top 5 trading partners buying the highest dollar worth of products exported from Poland are Germany, Czech Republic, France, United Kingdom and the Netherlands. Combined, that quintet of leading importers generated almost half (49%) of Polish exports by value.
From a continental perspective, 87.9% of Poland’s exports by value was delivered to fellow European countries while 5.5% was sold to importers in Asia.
Poland shipped another 4.3% worth of goods to customers in North America.
Smaller percentages went to buyers in Africa (1.3%), Latin America (0.6%) excluding Mexico but including the Caribbean, then Oceania (0.3%) led by Australia and New Zealand.
Poland has been a member of the European Union since May 1, 2004. Fellow EU members bought 75% of all products sold by Poland. That percentage lags 74.2% for 2024.
Poland’s Top Trading Partners
Below is a list showcasing 25 of Poland’s top trading partners in terms of exports sales. These are countries that imported the most Polish shipments by dollar value during 2025. Also shown is each import country’s percentage of total Polish exports.
- Germany: US$111.4 billion (26.9% of Poland’s total exports)
- Czech Republic: $25.9 billion (6.2%)
- France: $25 billion (6%)
- United Kingdom: $21.2 billion (5.1%)
- Netherlands: $19.1 billion (4.6%)
- Italy: $18.6 billion (4.5%)
- Ukraine: $14.8 billion (3.6%)
- United States: $13.1 billion (3.2%)
- Spain: $12.2 billion (3%)
- Slovakia: $11.2 billion (2.7%)
- Sweden: $10.9 billion (2.6%)
- Hungary: $10.5 billion (2.5%)
- Belgium: $9.4 billion (2.3%)
- Romania: $9.3 billion (2.3%)
- Austria: $8.3 billion (2%)
- Denmark: $7 billion (1.7%)
- Lithuania: $6.8 billion (1.6%)
- Türkiye: $5.4 billion (1.3%)
- Switzerland: $5.2 billion (1.3%)
- Norway: $4 billion (1%)
- mainland China: $3.5 billion (0.8%)
- Finland: $3.3 billion (0.8%)
- Canada: $2.95 billion (0.7%)
- Latvia: $2.88 billion (0.7%)
- Bulgaria: $2.7 billion (0.7%)
Approaching nine-tenths (88.1%) of Polish exports in 2025 was delivered to the above 25 trade partners.
The fastest-growing buyers of Poland’s exports from 2024 to 2025 were Canada (up 30.6%), Norway (up 24.6%), Sweden (up 20.3%), Bulgaria (up 13%) then Switzerland (up 11.8%).
The lone decliner was mainland China via a -2% slowdown from 2024.
Countries Generating Worst Trade Deficits for Poland
Poland experienced an overall -US$7 billion trade deficit in 2025, reversing an $837.1 million surplus one year earlier for 2024.
As defined by Investopedia, a country whose total value of all imported goods is higher than its value of all exports is said to have a negative trade balance or deficit.
It would be unrealistic for any exporting nation to expect across-the-board positive trade balances with all its importing partners. Similarly, that export country doesn’t necessarily post a negative trade balance with each individual partner with which it exchanges exports and imports.
Poland incurred the highest trade deficits with the following countries.
- mainland China: -US$61.7 billion (country-specific trade deficit in 2024)
- South Korea: -$10.4 billion
- Vietnam: -$7.1 billion
- United States: -$6.9 billion
- Saudi Arabia: -$5.9 billion
- Japan: -$4.9 billion
- Norway: -$4.5 billion
- Bangladesh: -$4.3 billion
- Türkiye: -$3.5 billion
- India: -$3.3 billion
Among Poland’s trading partners that cause the greatest negative trade balances, Polish deficits with Vietnam (up 36.1%), Türkiye (up 21.1%) and mainland China (up 19.8%) grew at the fastest pace from 2024 to 2025.
These cashflow deficiencies clearly indicate Poland’s competitive disadvantages with the above countries, but also represent key opportunities for Poland to develop country-specific strategies to strengthen its overall position in international trade.
Countries Generating Best Trade Surpluses for Poland
Based on Investopedia’s definition of net importer, a country whose total value of all imported goods is lower than its value of all exports is said to have a positive trade balance or surplus.
Poland earned the highest trade surpluses at the expense of the following countries.
- Germany: US$31.2 billion (country-specific trade surplus in 2024)
- United Kingdom: $12.4 billion
- Czech Republic: $11.9 billion
- France: $11 billion
- Ukraine: $9.9 billion
- Romania: $5.6 billion
- Slovakia: $5 billion
- Hungary: $4.3 billion
- Sweden: $3.6 billion
- Lithuania: $3.3 billion
Among Poland’s trading partners that generate the greatest positive trade balances, Polish surpluses with Sweden (up 35.7%), Hungary (up 16.1%) and France (up 10%) grew at the fastest pace from 2024 to 2025.
These positive cashflow streams clearly indicate Poland’s competitive advantages with the above countries, but also represent key opportunities for Poland to develop country-specific strategies to optimize its overall position in international trade.
Polish Export Companies Servicing Trading Partners
Seven corporations rank among Forbes Global 2000. Below is a sample of the major Polish companies that Forbes included.
- KGHM Polska Miedz (diversified metals, mining)
- Pgnig Group (oil, gas)
- PKN Orlen (oil, gas)
According to global trading platform Alibaba, the following companies are examples of export companies located in Poland. Shown within parenthesis is the product category that the Polish business specializes in.
- Ambra SA (sparking wines)
