National Suicide Prevention Lifeline is 1-800-273-8255.
Lifeline is 13 11 14.",How Syria's disinformation wars destroyed the co-founder of the White Helmets
d8ec22df-c6ff-4450-8486-5332e0a8303b,"coronavirus, NATO",7a0565e4-71fe-5ec4-b7b8-902e619a7e18,balkan insight,balkaninsight.com,https://balkaninsight.com/2020/01/13/montenegro-detainst-journalists-for-causing-panic/,English,17-04-2020,trustworthy,"Gojko Raicevic. Photo: IN4S
Police in Montenegro arrested the editors of two local news websites, IN4S and Borba, on suspicion of causing panic and public disorder in connection with reports of an explosion.
The Police Directorate on Sunday said Gojko Raicevic and Drazen Zivkovic had been arrested because they released information about an explosion at the Gorica state villa without checking the facts. Raicevic was later released while Zivkovic is being questioned in the prosecutor’s office.
“The state prosecutor has pleaded that there were elements of the crime of causing panic and disorder, and asked that they be arrested,” the police clarified.
IN4S and Borba both reported on Sunday that an explosion had occurred at the villa, which is used for official receptions, and that the security services suspected an attempted diversion.
In the meantime, some regional media also transmitted the information. The police later denied the report and said a minor failure of the electrical installations had occurred, which was soon repaired.
After his release, Raicevic said he had double-checked the information before it was published and that his arrest was really about intimidating journalists.
“It is clear to everyone that I was next after the arrest of the journalist Andela Djikanovic. I do not know who inspired the action by the police or prosecution. I think it will be known in the next few days, Raicevic said on Monday.
Journalists associations demanded that both Raicevic and Zivkovic be released and warned of violations of media freedom. The Centre for Investigative Reporting, CIN, said arrests of journalists were designed to limit human rights and freedoms and create a sense of legal uncertainty.
“The biggest producer of fake news in Montenegro is this government, which creates an atmosphere of fear and spreads panic about the country’s alleged defence against external enemies and its own citizens. Thus, the key problems of the state – lack of reforms, lack of rule of law and corruption at the highest level – are put under the rug,” CIN said in a statement.
Police also arrested Raicevic and Zivkovic after clashing with anti-government protesters in October 2015. Raicevic was also attacked during the protests, but the perpetrators were never brought to justice.
In July 2019, Raicevic was summoned for a police interview for publishing recordings of the telephone conversations of a former ambassador to Russia, Milan Rocen. His website published recordings of the former ambassador’s telephone calls from 2005 when he was Moscow ambassador for the then joint state of Serbia and Montenegro.
On 5 January, the editor-in-chief of web portal Fos media Andjela Djikanovic was placed in detention for 72 hours for “causing panic and disorder”, after claiming in an article that the government might call on the security forces in neighbouring Kosovo to help quell Serbian Church protests over a new religion law.
Police arrested her for claiming that members of the Kosovo Special Police were poised to intervene in Montenegro over Orthodox Christmas, which occurs a fortnight later than its Latin counterpart.
After the Council of Europe reported the arrest of the journalist, the Foreign Ministry insisted that the arrest did not affect media freedom in Montenegro, or compromise the security of journalists. They said Djikanovic should have checked the information before it was published.
“There are indications that the placement of this information was an attempt by a third party to interfere in Montenegro’s internal affairs with the intention of destabilizing the state and disturbing law and order,” the ministry said on January 10.",Montenegro Detains Journalists for “Causing Panic”
13d9532e-0cc8-48e4-82e4-7898b649624a,"Sanctions, Black Sea, food, War in Ukraine",2e853a07-9401-5aac-abfb-783e85d1ca25,business insider,businessinsider.com,https://www.businessinsider.com/russia-makes-sudden-decision-to-reverse-ukraine-grain-deal-suspension-2022-11,English,08-11-2022,trustworthy,"Russia abandoned its plan to block food exports leaving Ukraine on Wednesday, after a convoy of ships ignored President Vladimir Putin and sailed out of Ukraine anyway.
Moscow, which suspended its participation in the deal over the weekend, said in a statement on Wednesday, that it will resume operations again because it received assurances from Ukraine that it would not use the sea corridor for military purposes.
""Thanks to the involvement of the international organization, as well as the assistance of Turkey, we managed to obtain necessary written guarantees from Ukraine not to use the humanitarian corridor and Ukrainian ports ... for military actions against the Russian Federation,"" the statement said.
Turkish President Tayyip Erdogan, who helped broker the deal along with the UN, said Russia also told Turkey that operations would kick off again as of midday on Wednesday, Reuters reported.
Ukraine's Ministry of Defence did not immediately respond to Insider's request for comment.
The landmark grain deal between Moscow and Kyiv, which was brokered in July, was designed to keep agricultural products, including grain, moving out of Ukraine, which is a major food producer, amid the war.
But over the weekend, Russia said it would stop its involvement in the deal for an ""indefinite term"" after what it said was a Ukrainian drone attack on Russian naval ships in the Crimean port city of Sevastopol.
Without providing evidence, Russian officials said it could not ""guarantee the safety of civilian ships"" after the attack. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky called the move ""rather predictable,"" the BBC reported.
But despite the country's withdrawal, a UN official said Tuesday that they would press ahead with their plans to let authorized ships leave Ukraine.
On Tuesday, three cargo ships carrying corn, wheat, and sunflower oil were tracked leaving Ukrainian ports and traveling toward Istanbul, Ismini Palla, a UN spokeswoman told The New York Times, adding that Moscow had been notified of the departures.
Around 40% of the world's wheat supplies come from Ukraine, which is often referred to as the ""breadbasket of Europe.""
The agreement is critical to easing the global food crisis caused by the conflict, The Guardian reported, as food from Ukraine is exported around the world, including to drought-stricken nations as part of relief programs.
The World Food Programme warned earlier this year that Russia's invasion could result in a ""hunger catastrophe"" if such a food export deal was not struck.
The head of the European Council in June also accused Russia's troops of burning Ukrainian fields and stealing wheat, compounding food shortages.",Russia abandoned its plan to block food exports leaving Ukraine after ships ignored Putin and kept sailing anyway
