c0edb491-a34d-44d3-be92-3ba195d7c84a,"Kaliningrad, illegal annexation, NATO",b926a4af-97f3-50e3-8560-8757a2ef541d,the guardian,theguardian.com,https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2010/jul/31/kalingrad-kant-home-return-german,English,01-09-2021,trustworthy,"In May this year, the German weekly Der Spiegel published the sensational details of a little-known offer by the Soviet leadership to return Kaliningrad, the former Königsberg, to Germany in 1990. Though barely reported in the UK, the Spiegel article was widely discussed in the Russian media last month, leading to outcries of indignation by Russian nationalists and a subsequent denial that the return of Kaliningrad had ever been on the cards by no lesser person than the last leader of the Soviet Union, Mikhail Gorbachev.
The offer had been made in a secret telegram sent to the German embassy in Moscow, by one of the senior Soviet generals at the time, Geli Batenin. As Batenin is now dead, it is difficult to reconstruct exactly what was and what was not offered in the context of negotiations preparing for the reunification of Germany in 1990. Gorbachev, though, denies that Batenin was acting on behalf of the Soviet government. That should be taken with a pinch of salt, as there can be no doubt that the 1990 Soviet leadership was desperate to secure loans of billions of euros from the German government. The Germans, however, were lukewarm about the offer, allegedly telling the Soviets that reunification was the country's priority and that it regarded the Kaliningrad region as a Soviet problem.
Kaliningrad has a turbulent history. After two nights of Allied aerial bombardment in 1944, the German city of Königsberg, associated with towering German intellectuals, such as Immanuel Kant and Johann Gottfried Herder, was almost completely destroyed.
It suffered further. After the Red Army conquered the city in April 1945, the Soviets forcibly removed the remaining Germans from the territory in 1946, settled Russians in their place and renamed it Kaliningrad. The harbour near Kaliningrad, called Baltisk, became the only ice-free harbour of the Soviet Union and was therefore of major military significance during the cold war.
But there are many questions over it's future too. Kaliningrad is now an enclave surrounded by the EU states of Lithuania and Poland. History teaches us that enclaves are likely to be unstable both politically and geographically, so it is not by coincidence that modern state formation has resulted in their near-eradication in Europe. Kaliningrad is one of the few that remain.
Under its current governor, Georgi Boos, official policy underwritten by Moscow has been to strengthen the enclave's identification with the motherland. Kaliningraders, for example, are the only Russians to receive their passports for free. The region's school children are given free visits to the ""motherland"" – a unique privilege for Russian children. Their history lessons also emphasise the long-standing links of the region with Russia, however flimsy they might be.
Boos has even occasionally played up fears of re-Germanisation, alleging in 2006, for example, that Germany will never stop to in its efforts to get Kaliningrad back. But Boos' rhetoric has little anchorage in reality: there is absolutely no one in Germany, with the exception of a tiny extremist rightwing fringe, who call for the return of Kaliningrad. Germans fully accept that Königsberg has been permanently lost as a result of a war for which their country was responsible. In any case, the German government has no interest in creating a German exclave surrounded by Poland and Lithuania, both of which, incidentally, could also lay claim to Kaliningrad.
Lithuanians refer to the region as Lithuania Minor and regard it as cradle of the 19th-century Lithuanian national movement. And Poland, at times, has seen the Baltic region and large parts of Belarus as part of a greater Poland – sometimes going as far back as the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth of the 16th, 17th and 18th centuries.
But what about the Kaliningraders themselves? All survey data indicates that the vast majority see themselves as Russian, but Russian with a difference – more European. Kaliningrad intellectuals have been spearheading a rediscovery of the enclave's German past. Many are proud of their few German architectural remains and there has been vociferous support for the rebuilding of the German castle, destroyed by the Soviets. Some even support the renaming of Kaliningrad to Königsberg.
But almost no one in Kaliningrad wants to join Germany; even fewer feel any pro-Polish or pro-Lithuanian sentiments. There is a movement for greater autonomy of the region from Russia, but it is tiny. Only a handful of people speak of a fully independent fourth Baltic republic.
So at first sight, despite the contradictions and anomalies, the status quo is set to continue. But there is another factor at work which could be explosive . Much will depend on the future economic development of the region. Until the current economic crisis, Kaliningraders enjoyed economic growth and rising living standards. But in recent years, they have suffered much pain: in February this year, 10,000 Kaliningraders held a mass rally against what they perceived as basic social injustice.
If the situation deteriorates and Kaliningrad becomes an island of poverty and misery in the middle of a prosperous EU, the movement for territorial change will gather pace. But if Russia succeeds in saving the region from economic decline, Kaliningraders will continue to identify with Mother Russia first and foremost. Until we have a resolution on that front, no one can predict which way it will go.
