Mizintsev tipped off the Russian motive for his appearance, “We call on the United States of America to immediately stop the illegal occupation of the 55-kilometer zone around Al-Tanf, which is the sovereign territory of the Syrian Arab Republic.”
The statement indicated that talks between Jordan, Russia, and the US for the removal of Rukban’s residents, with many going to opposition-held northwest Syria, have not advanced.
Asked why the US-led coalition is not providing assistance to the camp, UK Maj. Gen. Chris Ghika said Tuesday:
The situation around Rukban is very complicated.As you know, there’s a deconfliction zone. There are a number of different forces operating there.
Rebels: Iran Trying to Undermine Demilitarized Zone in Northwest
Following an attack by pro-Assad forces, rebels have warned that Iran is trying to break up a demilitarized zone — declared by Russia and Turkey in mid-September — around opposition-held territory in northwest Syria.
Last week pro-Assad units assaulted a position of the Jaish al-Izza faction in northern Hama Province, with unconfirmed reports claiming 22 fatalities. Jaish al-Izza responded to the assault, regained its ground and firing on an Assad regime checkpoint. The jihadist bloc Hay’at Tahrir al-Sham also said it carried out retaliatory attacks.
Interviewed by On The Ground News, Jaish al-Izza spokesman Capt. Abu Salem confirmed the incident and some rebel “martyrs”. He distinguished between the regime’s two main allies: while Russia was still committed to the demilitarized zone, “it looks like Iran is trying to make this plan fail. It wants the resumption of war after it was paused for a bit.”
The captain continued, “It seems like [Iran] is trying to remind everybody that it is there after the sanctions made on it by the US.”
Pro-Assad forces have also periodically shelled the periphery of the zone, causing civilian casualties and bringing retaliatory fire from rebels.
But Russia has pushed back the regime’s attempts to suspend the zone, saying that Turkey is continuing to act in good faith to maintain the ceasefire.
