A journalist from LRT.lt was in Minsk and reported on the brutal crackdown by the Belarusian security forces.
On Monday, Lithuanian Prime Minister Saulius Skvernelis said that JedziÅskiâs statement was unacceptable but it did not represent the position of the political party that is part of Lithuaniaâs governing coalition.
âIt was an individual statement by the member of parliament. The coalition, LLRA-KÅ S have not declared a position differing from the Lithuanian position [â¦]. The political party should comment on its MPâs statement. I can only say that it was completely unacceptable and incomprehensible,â Skvernelis said.
Juozas Bernatonis, the head of the Seimasâ Committee on Foreign Affairs also said that by justifying police actions in Belarus JedziÅski demonstrated his lack of democratic values.
âThe force used against the peaceful protesters is disproportional. [The statement] shows that the person has no clue of what is happening in the neighbouring country and doesnât have the values that members of democratic parliaments should have,â Bernatonis told LRT RADIO.
The coalition leaders said that they were not planning to discipline JedziÅski but Skvernelis assured that LLRA-KÅ S could not remain in the government if it declared a similar position.
