ave used drones against civilian targets, including the former Chernobyl Nuclear Power Plant. The list of sanctioned persons also includes individuals and entities that are associated with the unlawful deportation and forced transfer of Ukrainian children within the territories of Ukraine temporarily occupied by Russia, the Kremlin’s information operations capabilities, senior Russian government officials, oligarchs and cronies who support the regime and members of Putin’s family. As well, Canada is updating and expanding the Restricted Goods and Technologies List to align with similar measures implemented by the European Union, the United Kingdom and the United States. Canada is also taking action against Russia’s ‘Shadow Fleet’ by listing 109 vessels based on their involvement in transporting property and goods for the benefit of Russia or persons in Russia. The list includes 92 oil tankers involved in transferring Russian oil to third countries, 9 liquefied natural gas (LNG) tankers involved in transferring Russian LNG to third countries, and 8 vessels involved in moving arms and related material to Russia from Iran and North Korea. On February 14, 2025, Canada further amended the Special Economic Measures (Russia) Regulations to add two (2) entities to Schedule 1 of the Regulations that are subsidiaries or affiliates of listed entities and have assisted the Russian government in evading sanctions. Canada also amended two criteria for listing persons under the Regulations: criterion 2a) to clarify that it is sufficient for a person to have engaged in sanctionable conduct at any point to be listed, and; criterion 2g) to clarify that former senior officials of listed entities can be sanctioned and further that the criterion also applies to senior officials of entities listed for conduct that supports the war or for participating in human rights violations.
