b8916f9f-5398-46ee-bfed-b85163dea78a,"Invasion of Ukraine, war crimes, War in Ukraine",5a83fca9-7768-528f-8a72-f0ded1a2e3b4,inews.co.uk,inews.co.uk,https://inews.co.uk/news/world/russia-war-crimes-ukraine-invasion-kindergarten-orphange-shelled-1484074,English,07-06-2022,trustworthy,"Russia has been accused of war crimes after harrowing reports it had shelled an orphanage and kindergarten on Friday morning during its invasion of Ukraine.
Ukraine’s Minister of Foreign Affairs, Dmytro Kuleba, said on Twitter that Moscow had committed war crimes and that evidence was being collected for a trial at The Hague. “Responsibility is inevitable,” he said.
Mr Kuleba did not disclose the location of the orphanage or kindergarten in question but one local said an orphanage in Volzel, outside Kyiv, had been shelled.
International Criminal Court (ICC) prosecutor Karim Khan on Friday expressed his concern over the Russian invasion of Ukraine and said his court might investigate possible war crimes in the country.
“I have been closely following recent developments in and around Ukraine with increasing concern,” Mr Khan said. “I remind all sides conducting hostilities on the territory of Ukraine that my office may exercise its jurisdiction and investigate any act of genocide, crime against humanity or war crime committed within Ukraine.”
Amnesty International research verified violations in international humanitarian law, saying that the Russian military had committed “indiscriminate” attacks during the invasion, in what the human rights organisation said could constitute war crimes.
It added that Russian claims to use precision guided weapons are patently false.
The humanitarian toll of Russia’s invasion of Ukraine is becoming increasingly visible as tanks roll in and buildings are targeted, with the UN estimating that more than 100,000 have left their homes as residents flee over borders into neighbouring countries.
AP journalist Francesca Ebel said Russia was continuing to bomb neighbourhoods as she posted a video of a bridge destroyed by Ukrainian authorities to prevent Russians from advancing.
Hundreds of people were crowded into a cramped bomb shelter beneath a building in Kyiv after a televised warning of air strikes.
“We don’t know how long we have to stay here. Good we have chairs at least,” said Viktoria, 35, while her children aged five and seven slept without taking off their winter coats. “We’re shocked… How can you wage a war against peaceful people?”
Alla, 40, said: “The kids were scared, they were crying and asking ‘Mom, will we all die?'”
“Ukrainians do not expect British or American boots on their soil,” Bohdan Nahaylo, editor of Ukraine’s English-language Kyiv Post, told i. “They will fight their own battles but they need their weapons badly, not to attack Russia but to defend their own borders.”
The 69-year-old warned that it is a “very dangerous moment” not just for Ukraine but for the world, after evacuating Kyiv for Barcelona.
He said the West and Nato can do “a lot more to help Ukraine survive this very difficult period”, starting with the immediate supply of arms, the “toughest” sanctions to “isolate and punish” Russia, as well as a “new architecture of security”.
Tens of thousands of Ukrainians, mostly women and children, crossed into Poland, Romania, Hungary and Slovakia on Friday as Russian missiles pounded the capital Kyiv and men of fighting age were told to remain.
Carloads of people waited in traffic jams for hours in freezing conditions as they rushed to leave Ukraine after Russian President Vladimir Putin launched the invasion.
Iryna, 36, and her mother set out from Kyiv on Thursday with her two girls aged two and four, before crossing into Ubla in Slovakia.
“We left my husband there, so he is still there supporting our government,” she said on Friday at a hotel in the border town of Snina. “We pray for Ukraine and I hope everything will be fine,” she said.
In Poland, which has the region’s largest Ukrainian community of about 1 million people, authorities said wait times to cross the border ranged from 6 to 12 hours in some places.
At Medyka in the south of Poland, 85 km (52 miles) from Lviv in western Ukraine, roads were packed with cars, police directing traffic, and people hugging loved ones after they arrived on the Polish side. An internet map site showed a third of the way congested with heavy traffic.
Ukrainian rules restrict men aged 18-60, who could be conscripted, from crossing the border. Marta Buach, 30, from Lviv, said her husband was not allowed to cross with her. “In Lviv it is OK but in other cities it is really a catastrophe. Kyiv was shelled, other small cities were shelled, we were hearing bombing everywhere.
“I think it is only a matter of time for it to be as dangerous as other cities,” she said.
United Nations aid agencies say the war could drive up to five million people to flee abroad. They said fuel, cash and medical supplies were running low in parts of Ukraine.
Border authorities said 29,000 people had entered Poland from Ukraine on Thursday, and about half had indicated they were fleeing the war. In Romania, more than 10,000 Ukrainians had arrived on Thursday, and nearly 3,000 in Slovakia.
Poland’s deputy interior minister Paweł Szefernaker said Ukrainian bus drivers were unable to drive across the border as conscription-age men were being held back in Ukraine.
Michał Mielniczuk, a spokesman for the southern Polish region of Podkarpackie, said temporary accommodation was being offered to people arriving.
“The vast majority continue on to other places throughout Poland after receiving a warm meal,” he told the PAP news agency.
On the border with northern Romania, women cried as they said goodbye to male loved ones, setting off to cross into Sighetu Marmatiei, a remote town on the banks of the Tisa river, a Reuters witness said.
Long queues had formed as cars waited to board a ferry over the Danube river into Isaccea, a town between Moldova and the Black Sea, local media in Romania showed.
Slovak authorities urged people to donate blood and set up hospitals with 5,380 beds assigned for the army or Nato use.",Russia accused of war crimes over shelling of kindergarten and orphanage during invasion
